; examples/macro-author.nx — what a user macro will look like
; when `defmacro` lands in Phase 3.
;
; Step #8c.3 closed the last piece: syntax-quote of vectors.
; The macroexpander can now synthesize the full Lisp shape
; library: `let*` bindings (vector), `fn*` params (vector),
; `loop*` bindings (vector), `cond` clauses (list of pairs).
;
; Today, host macros use direct FormBuilder. After #8d (Phase
; 3), users will write:
;
;   (defmacro unless [test & body]
;     \`(if ~test nil (do ~@body)))
;
; The runtime expansion is already proved out here — just
; without the (defmacro ...) sugar:

(let [name (quote my-binding) value 99]
  ; Construct (let* [my-binding 99] my-binding) — a fully
  ; valid nexis form ready to feed back into the compiler.
  `(let* [~name ~value] ~name))

; -> (let* [my-binding 99] my-binding)
